# Secure Your Agent

"A2A Your First Agent" built a working agent. This one locks it down:
authentication (who is calling), authority (what that caller may do), owner
scoping (callers only see their own rows), and card signing (how a client
trusts the card it discovered).

Authentication is NOT authority (RFC-SA2A-001 S29). A verified Bearer token
proves identity; a *grant* is what allows a consequential skill to run. The
two are configured separately and fail closed independently.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([{:ash, "~> 3.0"}, {:ash_a2a, "~> 26.10.3"}, {:plug, "~> 1.16"}],
  consolidate_protocols: false,
  config: [
    ash: [
      validate_domain_resource_inclusion?: false,
      validate_domain_config_inclusion?: false
    ]
  ]
)
```

## Define a note resource with an owner

`owner` is stamped from the caller's identity on create, and reads are
filtered to the caller. A caller with no identity sees no rows — dispatch
fails closed rather than trusting anything from message metadata.

```elixir
defmodule Tutorial.Note do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Tutorial.Domain,
    data_layer: Ash.DataLayer.Ets,
    extensions: [AshA2A]

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key(:id)
    attribute(:body, :string, allow_nil?: false, public?: true)
    attribute(:owner, :string, allow_nil?: false, public?: true)
  end

  actions do
    read :read do
      primary? true
      filter expr(owner == ^actor(:id))
    end

    create :create do
      accept([:body])

      change set_attribute(:owner, expr(^actor(:id)))
    end
  end

  a2a do
    skill(:create_note, :create, description: "Create a note")
    skill(:list_notes, :read)
  end
end
```

```elixir
defmodule Tutorial.Domain do
  use Ash.Domain, extensions: [AshA2A]

  resources do
    resource(Tutorial.Note)
  end
end

defmodule Tutorial.NoteAgent do
  use AshA2A.Agent, resource_or_domain: Tutorial.Domain, name: "tutorial_notes"
end

{:ok, _pid} = Tutorial.NoteAgent.start_link([])
```

## Authenticate: a verified credential becomes the actor

Identity only ever arrives from transport-verified auth — never from message
metadata. At the process level that is the `auth_identity` argument of
`AshA2A.Dispatcher.dispatch/6`; on the wire it is what
`AshA2A.Protocol.Plug.Auth` extracts from a real credential. Here is the
whole verification story: a callback you own.

```elixir
verify_callback = fn
  "bearer_auth", "valid-token-42", _conn -> {:ok, %{id: "user-42", tenant: "acme"}}
  "bearer_auth", "user-7s-token", _conn -> {:ok, %{id: "user-7", tenant: "acme"}}
  _scheme, _credential, _conn -> {:error, "invalid token"}
end
```

On the wire the same callback sits in the plug pipeline. Missing or rejected
credentials halt the conn with a `401` before `AshA2A.Protocol.Plug` ever
runs; a verified credential flows into `metadata["a2a.auth"]` and then into
`context.actor`/`context.tenant` inside your Ash action.

```elixir
schemes = %{"bearer_auth" => %AshA2A.Protocol.SecurityScheme.HTTPAuth{scheme: "bearer"}}
auth_opts = AshA2A.Protocol.Plug.Auth.init(schemes: schemes, verify: verify_callback)

# No credential: halted with 401.
anon_conn =
  Plug.Test.conn("post", "/", "")
  |> AshA2A.Protocol.Plug.Auth.call(auth_opts)

{anon_conn.status, anon_conn.resp_body}
#=> {401, "{\"error\":\"Unauthorized\"}"}
true = anon_conn.halted

# A valid credential: passes through, identity recorded on the conn.
authed_conn =
  Plug.Test.conn("post", "/", "")
  |> Plug.Conn.put_req_header("authorization", "bearer valid-token-42")
  |> AshA2A.Protocol.Plug.Auth.call(auth_opts)

false = authed_conn.halted
%{id: "user-42", tenant: "acme"} = authed_conn.private[:a2a][:auth][:identity]

# An invalid credential: halted with 401 too.
bad_conn =
  Plug.Test.conn("post", "/", "")
  |> Plug.Conn.put_req_header("authorization", "bearer forged-token")
  |> AshA2A.Protocol.Plug.Auth.call(auth_opts)

{bad_conn.status, true = bad_conn.halted}
```

## Authorize: consequential skills need a grant

`:change` skills (create/update/destroy) route through the receipted
`AshA2A.CommandBus`, which refuses any caller that does not hold a standing
grant for that exact capability. Grants are per `(principal, capability)`
pair: granting create grants create, never destroy. Make sure the `ash_a2a`
supervision tree (which hosts the authority broker) is running:

```elixir
{:ok, _apps} = Application.ensure_all_started(:ash_a2a)

create_msg =
  AshA2A.Protocol.Message.new_user([AshA2A.Protocol.Part.Data.new(%{"body" => "hello, securely"})])
  |> struct!(metadata: %{"skill" => "create_note"})

{:ok, task} =
  AshA2A.Protocol.call(Tutorial.NoteAgent, create_msg,
    metadata: %{"a2a.auth" => %{identity: %{id: "user-42", tenant: "acme"}}}
  )

task.status.state
#=> :failed
```

The refusal is typed, not a mystery. The failed task carries the
`:authority_required` refusal code, and nothing was written, executed, or
receipted.

```elixir
AshA2A.Protocol.Message.text(task.status.message)
#=> "Error: %{code: :authority_required}"
```

Now issue the standing grant — the subject must be built from the same
identity term your `verify` callback returns — and retry the same call:

```elixir
user_42 = AshA2A.Identity.principal(%{id: "user-42", tenant: "acme"})

{:ok, _authority} = AshA2A.Authority.Grant.grant(user_42, "Tutorial.Note.create")
true = AshA2A.Authority.Grant.granted?(user_42, "Tutorial.Note.create")
false = AshA2A.Authority.Grant.granted?(user_42, "Tutorial.Note.update")

{:ok, task} =
  AshA2A.Protocol.call(Tutorial.NoteAgent, create_msg,
    metadata: %{"a2a.auth" => %{identity: %{id: "user-42", tenant: "acme"}}}
  )

task.status.state
#=> :completed
```

## Owner scoping: callers only see their own rows

Identity flows into the Ash action as `context.actor`, so plain Ash
authorization patterns — here a preparation filter — scope every read. This
dispatch passes `auth_identity` directly, the process-level form of what
`Plug.Auth` injects on the wire.

```elixir
# A second note, created fresh (an identical message id would replay the
# stored receipt instead of writing a new row).
scoped_msg =
  AshA2A.Protocol.Message.new_user([AshA2A.Protocol.Part.Data.new(%{"body" => "user-42's second note"})])
  |> struct!(metadata: %{"skill" => "create_note"})

{:ok, task} =
  AshA2A.Protocol.call(Tutorial.NoteAgent, scoped_msg,
    metadata: %{"a2a.auth" => %{identity: %{id: "user-42", tenant: "acme"}}}
  )

:completed = task.status.state

list_msg = fn identity ->
  AshA2A.Protocol.Message.new_user([AshA2A.Protocol.Part.Data.new(%{})])
  |> struct!(metadata: %{"skill" => "list_notes"})
end

{:reply, [own]} =
  AshA2A.Dispatcher.dispatch("list_notes", list_msg.(nil), Tutorial.Domain, [], %{id: "user-42", tenant: "acme"})

{:reply, [others]} =
  AshA2A.Dispatcher.dispatch("list_notes", list_msg.(nil), Tutorial.Domain, [], %{id: "user-7", tenant: "acme"})

# No identity at all: the action refuses (`actor is required`) rather than
# returning an empty page — fail closed.
{:input_required, [_prompt]} =
  AshA2A.Dispatcher.dispatch("list_notes", list_msg.(nil), Tutorial.Domain)

length(own.data[:results])
#=> 1

others.data[:results]
#=> []
```

Revocation is the symmetric counterpart, and the next dispatch fails closed:

```elixir
:ok = AshA2A.Authority.Grant.revoke(user_42, "Tutorial.Note.create")
false = AshA2A.Authority.Grant.granted?(user_42, "Tutorial.Note.create")
```

## Card signing: trust the card you discovered

A discovered card is only as trustworthy as its transport. Sign the card
server-side and verify the detached JWS client-side — `verify/2` recomputes
the JCS digest of the wire-projected card and checks every `signatures`
entry.

```elixir
card = AshA2A.Info.agent_card(Tutorial.Domain)
signing_key = "a-real-secret-key"

signed = AshA2A.Protocol.CardSigning.sign(card, signing_key)
:ok = AshA2A.Protocol.CardSigning.verify(signed, signing_key)
```

Tampering with the payload breaks the digest; a different key breaks the
signature. Both fail closed:

```elixir
{:error, {:digest_mismatch, _}} =
  AshA2A.Protocol.CardSigning.verify(%{signed | description: "tampered"}, signing_key)

{:error, {:bad_signature, _}} =
  AshA2A.Protocol.CardSigning.verify(signed, "a-different-key")
```

## What you built, and what's next

You now have an agent that authenticates callers with a callback you own,
refuses ungranted consequential calls with a typed `:authority_required`,
scopes reads to their owners, and signs its card for wire clients.

From here:

- [Stream and Elicit](stream-and-elicit.livemd) for lazy reads and
  `:input_required` resumes;
- [Authenticate inbound A2A requests](../docs/how-to/authenticate-agent-requests.md)
  for the full trust-boundary discussion, broker backends, and the async
  (Oban) re-verification requirement;
- [A2A endpoint reference](../docs/reference/a2a-endpoint-contract.md) for
  error codes and metadata merge order.
